# ===== 05-design-implementation.md =====
$path = Join-Path $base "05-design-implementation.md"
$c = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($path, $utf8)
$c = $c.Replace("# Design and Implementation`n`n", "# Design and Implementation`n`n> **Previous:** [Architectural Design](./04-architectural-design.md) | **Next:** [Software Testing](./06-testing.md)`n`n")
$o = "and describe the role of integrated development environments in implementation."
$g = "`n`n## Chapter at a Glance`n`n| Topic | Key Insight | Practical Takeaway |`n|-------|-------------|-------------------|`n| SOLID Principles | Five principles for OO class design | SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, DIP guide maintainable design |`n| DRY KISS YAGNI | Simplicity and avoiding duplication | Do not repeat, keep it simple, you aint gonna need it |`n| Coupling and Cohesion | Interdependence and internal focus | Aim for low coupling and high cohesion |`n| Information Hiding | Hide volatile decisions behind interfaces | Reduces ripple effect of changes |`n| Design Review | Systematic evaluation of design | Catch design defects before implementation |`n`n## Chapter Roadmap`n`n```mermaid`nflowchart LR`n    A[Design Principles] --> B[SOLID]`n    A --> C[DRY KISS YAGNI]`n    A --> D[Separation of Concerns]`n    A --> E[Information Hiding]`n    A --> F[Coupling and Cohesion]`n    A --> G[Design Heuristics]`n    A --> H[Design Review]`n    B --> I[SRP OCP LSP ISP DIP]`n```"
$c = $c.Replace($o, $o + $g)
$c = $c.Replace("### Design Principles`n`nDesign principles are established guidelines", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Design principles are distilled experience that guides maintainable, understandable software.`n`n### Design Principles`n`nDesign principles are established guidelines")
$c = $c.Replace("### The SOLID Principles`n`nThe SOLID principles, articulated by Robert C. Martin", "> **Pro Tip:** SRP is the most impactful SOLID principle - a class with one reason to change is easier to understand, test, and maintain.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** SOLID provides five principles for robust OO design: SRP, OCP, LSP, ISP, DIP.`n`n### The SOLID Principles`n`nThe SOLID principles, articulated by Robert C. Martin")
$c = $c.Replace("### DRY, KISS, and YAGNI`n`nThe DRY principle", "> **Warning:** DRY can be overapplied - premature abstraction to avoid duplication can increase complexity more than the duplication itself.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** DRY avoids duplication, KISS favours simplicity, YAGNI defers functionality until needed.`n`n### DRY, KISS, and YAGNI`n`nThe DRY principle")
$c = $c.Replace("### Separation of Concerns`n`nSeparation of concerns is the principle", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Separation of concerns partitions the system so each section addresses a distinct concern independently.`n`n### Separation of Concerns`n`nSeparation of concerns is the principle")
$c = $c.Replace("### Information Hiding`n`nInformation hiding, articulated by Parnas", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Information hiding conceals changeable design decisions behind stable interfaces - reducing the ripple effect.`n`n### Information Hiding`n`nInformation hiding, articulated by Parnas")
$c = $c.Replace("### Coupling and Cohesion`n`nCoupling measures the degree of interdependence", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Low coupling and high cohesion are the primary indicators of design quality; they tend to correlate.`n`n### Coupling and Cohesion`n`nCoupling measures the degree of interdependence")
$c = $c.Replace("### Design Heuristics`n`nHeuristics are experience-based guidelines", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Design heuristics complement formal principles; the Law of Demeter and composition over inheritance are key examples.`n`n### Design Heuristics`n`nHeuristics are experience-based guidelines")
$c = $c.Replace("### Design Review`n`nDesign review is a systematic evaluation", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Design review checklists verify correctness, completeness, consistency, and adherence to standards.`n`n### Design Review`n`nDesign review is a systematic evaluation")
$c = $c.Replace("### Implementation Issues`n`nSoftware reuse is the practice", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Reuse, configuration management, build tools, and IDEs are essential implementation infrastructure.`n`n### Implementation Issues`n`nSoftware reuse is the practice")
$s = "## Summary`n`nDesign principles guide the creation"
$r = "## Concept Comparison Table`n`n| Principle | Focus | Key Benefit | Risk if Violated |`n|-----------|-------|-------------|------------------|`n| SRP | Single reason to change | Easier maintenance | Fragile classes |`n| OCP | Extend without modifying | Non-invasive enhancement | Rigid code |`n| LSP | Substitutability | Correct polymorphism | Broken contracts |`n| ISP | Small focused interfaces | Decoupled clients | Bloated interfaces |`n| DIP | Depend on abstractions | Testable architecture | Tight coupling |`n| DRY | No duplication | Consistency | Repeated changes |`n| YAGNI | Build only what is needed | Lean codebase | Wasted effort |`n`n## Quick Reference`n`n| Concept | Definition | Practical Example |`n|---------|------------|-------------------|`n| Coupling | Inter-module dependence | Data coupling preferred over content |`n| Cohesion | Intra-module focus | Functional cohesion is ideal |`n| Information Hiding | Encapsulate volatility | Hide DB details behind repository |`n| Law of Demeter | Talk only to immediate friends | Dont chain method calls |`n| Design Review | Systematic evaluation | Checklist-based inspection |`n`n## Cross-Application Matrix`n`n| Domain | Key Principles | Most Critical Review Aspect |`n|--------|---------------|---------------------------|`n| Banking | DIP, SRP, Security | Correctness and auditability |`n| E-commerce | OCP, ISP, YAGNI | Extensibility |`n| Embedded | LSP, Information Hiding | Memory and timing correctness |`n| Mobile | SRP, YAGNI | Performance and testability |`n| Data Science | DRY, Modularity | Reproducibility |`n`n## Chapter Quiz`n`n1. Which SOLID principle states that high-level modules should not depend on low-level modules?`n   A) SRP`n   B) OCP`n   C) DIP`n   D) ISP`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** DIP - both should depend on abstractions, not on concrete implementations.</details>`n`n2. What does YAGNI stand for?`n   A) You Always Get New Ideas`n   B) You Aint Gonna Need It`n   C) Yet Another Generalised Network Interface`n   D) Your Application Generates New Issues`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**B)** YAGNI warns against building functionality before it is actually required.</details>`n`n3. Which coupling type is most desirable?`n   A) Content coupling`n   B) Common coupling`n   C) Data coupling`n   D) Control coupling`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** Data coupling - modules share data only through parameters.</details>`n`n4. Which cohesion type is considered ideal?`n   A) Coincidental`n   B) Logical`n   C) Sequential`n   D) Functional`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**D)** Functional cohesion - all elements contribute to a single, well-defined function.</details>`n`n## Summary`n`nDesign principles guide the creation"
$c = $c.Replace($s, $r)
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $c, $utf8)
Write-Host "OK 05-design-implementation.md"

# ===== 06-testing.md =====
$path = Join-Path $base "06-testing.md"
$c = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($path, $utf8)
$c = $c.Replace("# Software Testing`n`n", "# Software Testing`n`n> **Previous:** [Design and Implementation](./05-design-implementation.md) | **Next:** [Software Evolution](./07-software-evolution.md)`n`n")
$o = "and explain test-driven development and behaviour-driven development."
$g = "`n`n## Chapter at a Glance`n`n| Topic | Key Insight | Practical Takeaway |`n|-------|-------------|-------------------|`n| V&V | Verification vs Validation | Verification builds it right; validation builds the right thing |`n| Test Levels | Unit, Integration, System, Acceptance | Each level targets different defects |`n| White-Box | Statement, Branch, Path, Condition coverage | Path coverage is strongest but often impractical |`n| Black-Box | Equivalence partitioning, BVA, decision tables | BVA catches boundary defects effectively |`n| TDD | Red-Green-Refactor cycle | Test-first produces testable, well-structured code |`n`n## Chapter Roadmap`n`n```mermaid`nflowchart LR`n    A[Software Testing] --> B[Verification]`n    A --> C[Validation]`n    B --> D[Unit Testing]`n    B --> E[Integration Testing]`n    B --> F[System Testing]`n    C --> G[Acceptance Testing]`n    D --> H[White-Box]`n    D --> I[Black-Box]`n    H --> J[Statement Branch Path]`n    I --> K[Equiv BVA Decision Table]`n```"
$c = $c.Replace($o, $o + $g)
$c = $c.Replace("### Verification and Validation`n`nVerification and validation (V&V) are the two principal approaches", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Verification checks conformance to specification; validation checks satisfaction of actual stakeholder needs.`n`n### Verification and Validation`n`nVerification and validation (V&V) are the two principal approaches")
$c = $c.Replace("### Levels of Testing`n`nTesting is organised into levels", "> **Remember:** The V-model pairs each development phase with a corresponding test level, ensuring traceability from requirements to acceptance testing.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Testing spans four levels: unit, integration, system, and acceptance.`n`n### Levels of Testing`n`nTesting is organised into levels")
$c = $c.Replace("### Functional Testing`n`nFunctional testing verifies that the system", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Functional testing validates system behaviour against specifications using black-box techniques.`n`n### Functional Testing`n`nFunctional testing verifies that the system")
$c = $c.Replace("### Structural Testing`n`nStructural testing, also known as white-box testing", "> **Pro Tip:** Branch coverage subsumes statement coverage - aim for 100% branch coverage as a practical minimum target.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** White-box testing uses code structure; branch coverage is stronger than statement coverage.`n`n### Structural Testing`n`nStructural testing, also known as white-box testing")
$c = $c.Replace("### Black-Box Testing Techniques`n`nEquivalence partitioning divides the input domain", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Equivalence partitioning and boundary value analysis are complementary - BVA catches boundary defects that partitioning misses.`n`n### Black-Box Testing Techniques`n`nEquivalence partitioning divides the input domain")
$c = $c.Replace("### Non-Functional Testing`n`nPerformance testing evaluates system behaviour", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Non-functional testing addresses performance, security, and usability - often more critical than functional correctness.`n`n### Non-Functional Testing`n`nPerformance testing evaluates system behaviour")
$c = $c.Replace("### Regression Testing`n`nRegression testing verifies that changes", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Regression testing prevents reintroduction of defects; automation makes frequent execution feasible.`n`n### Regression Testing`n`nRegression testing verifies that changes")
$c = $c.Replace("### Smoke Testing`n`nSmoke testing, also called build verification testing", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Smoke tests quickly determine whether a build is stable enough for further testing.`n`n### Smoke Testing`n`nSmoke testing, also called build verification testing")
$c = $c.Replace("### Test Automation`n`nTest automation uses software tools", "> **Pro Tip:** Follow the test automation pyramid - invest most in unit tests, fewer in API tests, fewest in UI end-to-end tests.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** The test automation pyramid recommends many unit tests, fewer service tests, and fewest UI tests.`n`n### Test Automation`n`nTest automation uses software tools")
$c = $c.Replace("### Test-Driven Development`n`nTest-driven development (TDD) reverses the traditional order", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** TDDs red-green-refactor cycle produces comprehensive unit tests and drives clean, testable design.`n`n### Test-Driven Development`n`nTest-driven development (TDD) reverses the traditional order")
$c = $c.Replace("### Behaviour-Driven Development`n`nBehaviour-driven development (BDD) extends TDD", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** BDD uses Given-When-Then format for shared understanding between developers, testers, and domain experts.`n`n### Behaviour-Driven Development`n`nBehaviour-driven development (BDD) extends TDD")
$s = "## Summary`n`nSoftware testing is the primary dynamic"
$r = "## Concept Comparison Table`n`n| Technique | Type | Coverage Criterion | Best For |`n|-----------|------|-------------------|----------|`n| Statement Coverage | White-box | Each statement executed | Basic safety net |`n| Branch Coverage | White-box | Each decision outcome exercised | Practical minimum |`n| Path Coverage | White-box | All execution paths | Safety-critical (impractical) |`n| Equivalence Partitioning | Black-box | One value per class | Reducing test count |`n| Boundary Value Analysis | Black-box | Boundary values | Finding boundary defects |`n| Decision Table | Black-box | All condition combinations | Complex business logic |`n| State Transition | Black-box | States and transitions | Control-intensive systems |`n`n## Quick Reference`n`n| Concept | Definition | Application |`n|---------|------------|-------------|`n| Verification | Building it right | Conformance to spec |`n| Validation | Building the right thing | Satisfaction of needs |`n| TDD | Test-first development | Red-green-refactor cycle |`n| BDD | Behaviour-driven tests | Given-When-Then format |`n| Regression | Re-testing after changes | Prevent defect reintroduction |`n| Smoke | Quick build sanity check | Build acceptance |`n`n## Cross-Application Matrix`n`n| Domain | Primary Technique | Automation Priority |`n|--------|-------------------|-------------------|`n| Healthcare | Decision Table, Path Coverage | High (regulatory) |`n| E-commerce | BVA, Equivalence Partitioning | High (frequent changes) |`n| Embedded | State Transition, Branch Coverage | Medium |`n| Mobile | Smoke, BVA, Regression | High (many devices) |`n| Finance | Decision Table, Security Testing | Critical (audit) |`n`n## Chapter Quiz`n`n1. What is the difference between verification and validation?`n   A) Verification is automated; validation is manual`n   B) Verification checks conformance to spec; validation checks user needs`n   C) Verification is for code; validation is for requirements`n   D) There is no difference`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**B)** Verification: are we building the product right? Validation: are we building the right product?</details>`n`n2. Which coverage criterion subsumes statement coverage?`n   A) Path coverage`n   B) Branch coverage`n   C) Condition coverage`n   D) All of the above`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**D)** Path, branch, and condition coverage all subsume statement coverage.</details>`n`n3. According to the test automation pyramid, which layer should have the most tests?`n   A) UI end-to-end tests`n   B) API service tests`n   C) Unit tests`n   D) Manual tests`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** Unit tests form the broad base of the pyramid - they are fast, reliable, and cheap to maintain.</details>`n`n4. What does the acronym TDD stand for?`n   A) Test-Driven Documentation`n   B) Test-Driven Development`n   C) Technical Design Document`n   D) Type-Driven Design`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**B)** TDD stands for Test-Driven Development - write failing test first, then code, then refactor.</details>`n`n## Summary`n`nSoftware testing is the primary dynamic"
$c = $c.Replace($s, $r)
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $c, $utf8)
Write-Host "OK 06-testing.md"

# ===== 07-software-evolution.md =====
$path = Join-Path $base "07-software-evolution.md"
$c = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($path, $utf8)
$c = $c.Replace("# Software Evolution`n`n", "# Software Evolution`n`n> **Previous:** [Software Testing](./06-testing.md) | **Next:** [Project Management](./08-project-management.md)`n`n")
$o = "and articulate Lehman's laws of software evolution."
$g = "`n`n## Chapter at a Glance`n`n| Topic | Key Insight | Practical Takeaway |`n|-------|-------------|-------------------|`n| Maintenance Types | Corrective, Adaptive, Perfective, Preventive | Perfective maintenance consumes most effort |`n| Reverse Engineering | Extract design from existing code | Essential when documentation is outdated |`n| Refactoring | Restructure without changing behaviour | Small steps with frequent testing |`n| Legacy Systems | Old systems still critical | Strategies: scrap, freeze, maintain, transform, wrap |`n| Lehman's Laws | Eight empirical evolution laws | Complexity increases without deliberate refactoring |`n`n## Chapter Roadmap`n`n```mermaid`nflowchart LR`n    A[Software Evolution] --> B[Corrective Maintenance]`n    A --> C[Adaptive Maintenance]`n    A --> D[Perfective Maintenance]`n    A --> E[Preventive Maintenance]`n    A --> F[Reverse Engineering]`n    A --> G[Refactoring]`n    A --> H[Legacy Systems]`n    A --> I[Lehman Laws]`n    F --> J[Reengineering]`n    H --> K[Wrap Scrap Maintain Transform]`n```"
$c = $c.Replace($o, $o + $g)
$c = $c.Replace("### The Nature of Software Evolution`n`nSoftware evolution is the continuous process", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Software must evolve or become irrelevant; maintenance costs typically exceed initial development costs.`n`n### The Nature of Software Evolution`n`nSoftware evolution is the continuous process")
$c = $c.Replace("### Categories of Maintenance`n`nSoftware maintenance is conventionally classified", "> **Remember:** Perfective maintenance (enhancements) consumes the largest proportion of maintenance effort - typically over 50%.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** The four maintenance types are corrective, adaptive, perfective, and preventive.`n`n### Categories of Maintenance`n`nSoftware maintenance is conventionally classified")
$c = $c.Replace("### Reverse Engineering`n`nReverse engineering is the process of analysing", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Reverse engineering recovers design information from code without changing the system; reengineering adds changes.`n`n### Reverse Engineering`n`nReverse engineering is the process of analysing")
$c = $c.Replace("### Refactoring`n`nRefactoring is the process of restructuring existing code", "> **Pro Tip:** Apply the boy-scout rule - leave the code cleaner than you found it. Small, continuous refactoring prevents structural decay.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Refactoring preserves external behaviour while improving internal structure through small, tested transformations.`n`n### Refactoring`n`nRefactoring is the process of restructuring existing code")
$c = $c.Replace("### Legacy Systems`n`nLegacy systems are older systems", "> **Warning:** Legacy system replacement is risky - the business rules embedded in a 30-year-old system are often not fully documented or understood.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Legacy management strategies include scrap, freeze, maintain, transform, and wrap - chosen by business value and technical quality.`n`n### Legacy Systems`n`nLegacy systems are older systems")
$c = $c.Replace("### Regression Testing During Evolution`n`nRegression testing is essential during software evolution", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Regression test suites face obsolescence, fragility, and bloat - prioritisation and selection strategies are essential.`n`n### Regression Testing During Evolution`n`nRegression testing is essential during software evolution")
$c = $c.Replace("### Lehman's Laws of Software Evolution`n`nLehman formulated eight laws", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Lehman's laws describe evolution dynamics: continuing change, increasing complexity, self-regulation, and feedback systems.`n`n### Lehman's Laws of Software Evolution`n`nLehman formulated eight laws")
$s = "## Summary`n`nSoftware evolution consumes the majority"
$r = "## Concept Comparison Table`n`n| Maintenance Type | Purpose | Example | Effort Share |`n|-----------------|---------|---------|--------------|`n| Corrective | Fix defects | Patch a null-pointer bug | ~20% |`n| Adaptive | Respond to environment | Upgrade for new OS version | ~25% |`n| Perfective | Enhance functionality | Add a new report feature | ~50% |`n| Preventive | Prevent future problems | Refactor to reduce complexity | ~5% |`n`n## Quick Reference`n`n| Concept | Definition | Key Technique |`n|---------|------------|---------------|`n| Reverse Engineering | Analyse code to recover design | Static analysis, dependency graphs |`n| Reengineering | Reverse + forward engineering | Extract, transform, regenerate |`n| Refactoring | Restructure without behaviour change | Extract Method, Rename, Move Class |`n| Legacy System | Old critical system | Portfolio analysis for strategy |`n| Strangler Fig | Incrementally replace legacy | Route features to new system gradually |`n`n## Cross-Application Matrix`n`n| Scenario | Recommended Strategy | Key Risk |`n|----------|----------------------|----------|`n| High business value, low technical quality | Reengineer | Business disruption |`n| Low business value, low technical quality | Scrap and replace | Data migration |`n| High business value, high technical quality | Continue evolution | Complacency |`n| Low business value, high technical quality | Maintain | Cost exceeds benefit |`n`n## Chapter Quiz`n`n1. Which maintenance category consumes the largest share of effort?`n   A) Corrective`n   B) Adaptive`n   C) Perfective`n   D) Preventive`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** Perfective maintenance (enhancements) accounts for approximately 50% of maintenance effort.</details>`n`n2. What is the key constraint when refactoring code?`n   A) It must add new features`n   B) External behaviour must not change`n   C) It must reduce lines of code`n   D) It must be done by senior developers`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**B)** Refactoring restructures code without changing its observable external behaviour.</details>`n`n3. Which Lehman law states that system complexity increases unless work is done to reduce it?`n   A) Law of Continuing Change`n   B) Law of Increasing Complexity`n   C) Law of Declining Quality`n   D) Law of Conservation of Familiarity`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**B)** The Law of Increasing Complexity warns that entropy increases without deliberate refactoring.</details>`n`n## Summary`n`nSoftware evolution consumes the majority"
$c = $c.Replace($s, $r)
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $c, $utf8)
Write-Host "OK 07-software-evolution.md"

# ===== 08-project-management.md =====
$path = Join-Path $base "08-project-management.md"
$c = [System.IO.File]::ReadAllText($path, $utf8)
$c = $c.Replace("# Project Management`n`n", "# Project Management`n`n> **Previous:** [Software Evolution](./07-software-evolution.md) | **Next:** [Quality Management](./09-quality-management.md)`n`n")
$o = "and explain earned value management for project tracking."
$g = "`n`n## Chapter at a Glance`n`n| Topic | Key Insight | Practical Takeaway |`n|-------|-------------|-------------------|`n| Project Planning | Scope, WBS, estimation, schedule | Start with a clear project charter and WBS |`n| Estimation | Expert judgement, FP, COCOMO | Use multiple techniques and express as ranges |`n| Scheduling | Gantt, PERT, CPM | Identify the critical path |`n| Risk Management | Identify, analyse, plan, monitor | Proactive risk management prevents problems |`n| Earned Value | PV, EV, AC integrated | CPI < 1 means over budget; SPI < 1 means behind |`n`n## Chapter Roadmap`n`n```mermaid`nflowchart LR`n    A[Project Management] --> B[Planning]`n    A --> C[Estimation]`n    A --> D[Scheduling]`n    A --> E[Risk Management]`n    A --> F[Team Organisation]`n    A --> G[Earned Value]`n    B --> H[WBS Charter]`n    C --> I[Function Points COCOMO]`n    D --> J[Gantt PERT CPM]`n    E --> K[Identify Analyse Mitigate]`n```"
$c = $c.Replace($o, $o + $g)
$c = $c.Replace("### The Nature of Software Project Management`n`nSoftware project management is the discipline", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Software projects face high uncertainty; effective management balances scope, time, cost, and quality.`n`n### The Nature of Software Project Management`n`nSoftware project management is the discipline")
$c = $c.Replace("### Project Planning`n`nProject planning begins with defining", "> **Pro Tip:** A well-structured WBS decomposing work to 3+ levels is the foundation of reliable estimation and scheduling.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Project planning starts with a charter and WBS, decomposing work into manageable units.`n`n### Project Planning`n`nProject planning begins with defining")
$c = $c.Replace("### Estimation`n`nSoftware estimation is the process", "> **Warning:** Estimates are inherently uncertain - always express them as ranges, not single-point numbers, and update as understanding improves.`n`n> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Estimation combines expert judgment (Delphi, planning poker) with algorithmic models (function points, COCOMO).`n`n### Estimation`n`nSoftware estimation is the process")
$c = $c.Replace("### Scheduling`n`nScheduling assigns time to the work", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** PERT uses three-point estimation; CPM identifies the critical path determining minimum project duration.`n`n### Scheduling`n`nScheduling assigns time to the work")
$c = $c.Replace("### Risk Management`n`nRisk management is the systematic process", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Risk management is proactive - identify, analyse, prioritise, plan responses, and monitor throughout the project.`n`n### Risk Management`n`nRisk management is the systematic process")
$c = $c.Replace("### Team Organisation`n`nSoftware development teams can be organised", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** Team structures range from chief programmer to democratic models; agile teams are small, cross-functional, and self-organising.`n`n### Team Organisation`n`nSoftware development teams can be organised")
$c = $c.Replace("### Earned Value Analysis`n`nEarned value analysis (EVA) integrates scope", "> **One-Sentence Takeaway:** EVA integrates scope, schedule, and cost - SPI < 1 means behind schedule; CPI < 1 means over budget.`n`n### Earned Value Analysis`n`nEarned value analysis (EVA) integrates scope")
$s = "## Summary`n`nSoftware project management addresses"
$r = "## Concept Comparison Table`n`n| Technique | Input | Output | Best Use |`n|-----------|-------|--------|----------|`n| Function Points | Function types and complexity | FP count (language-independent) | Early estimation from requirements |`n| COCOMO II | Size, scale factors, cost drivers | Effort in person-months | Detailed estimation with context |`n| PERT | Three time estimates per activity | Expected duration, critical path | Uncertain schedules |`n| CPM | Single duration estimates | Critical path, float | Deterministic schedules |`n| Gantt Chart | Activities and durations | Visual timeline | Communicating the schedule |`n`n## Quick Reference`n`n| Concept | Definition | Formula |`n|---------|------------|--------|`n| WBS | Hierarchical decomposition of work | N/A |`n| EV | Budgeted cost of work performed | Sum of planned costs of completed work |`n| SV | Schedule Variance | EV - PV |`n| CV | Cost Variance | EV - AC |`n| SPI | Schedule Performance Index | EV / PV |`n| CPI | Cost Performance Index | EV / AC |`n| Critical Path | Longest path through project network | Determines minimum project duration |`n`n## Cross-Application Matrix`n`n| Project Type | Estimation Method | Scheduling Focus | Key Risk |`n|-------------|-------------------|------------------|----------|`n| Embedded systems | COCOMO II | Task dependencies | Hardware delays |`n| Web application | Planning poker (agile) | Sprint planning | Changing requirements |`n| Enterprise ERP | Function Points + COCOMO | Critical path | Integration complexity |`n| Mobile app | Story points + velocity | Release planning | Platform fragmentation |`n| Safety-critical | COCOMO II detailed | Milestone tracking | Regulatory compliance |`n`n## Chapter Quiz`n`n1. What does CPI < 1.0 indicate?`n   A) Ahead of schedule`n   B) Under budget`n   C) Over budget`n   D) Behind schedule`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** CPI (Cost Performance Index) below 1.0 means the project is over budget.</details>`n`n2. Which scheduling technique uses three time estimates (optimistic, most likely, pessimistic)?`n   A) CPM`n   B) Gantt`n   C) PERT`n   D) WBS`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** PERT uses three-point estimation to calculate expected duration.</details>`n`n3. What is the critical path in a project network?`n   A) The shortest path`n   B) The path with the most resources`n   C) The longest path determining minimum duration`n   D) The path with the highest risk`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**C)** The critical path is the longest path through the network and determines the minimum project duration.</details>`n`n4. What are the three categories of risk response?`n   A) Avoid, mitigate, accept`n   B) Plan, execute, monitor`n   C) Identify, analyse, respond`n   D) Transfer, ignore, defer`n`n<details><summary>Answer</summary>**A)** Risk response strategies include avoidance, mitigation (including transfer), and acceptance.</details>`n`n## Summary`n`nSoftware project management addresses"
$c = $c.Replace($s, $r)
[System.IO.File]::WriteAllText($path, $c, $utf8)
Write-Host "OK 08-project-management.md"

Write-Host "=== Files 05-08 DONE ==="
